    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d10">

                <pb facs="0113" n="17" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg17"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.3731/202</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Cuba</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Crowder</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-08-21"><hi rendition="#italic">August 21,
                        1926</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 781</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department refers to your despatches
                    No. 1416 of May 7, 1926, and No. 1462 of June 10, 1926,<anchor n="9"
                        xml:id="d10-f1"/> relating to the proposal of the Cuban Government for a
                    revision of the Reciprocity Convention of 1902.</p>
                <p>The Department has received from the United States Tariff Commission a letter
                    dated June 12, 1926,<anchor n="10" xml:id="d10-f2"/> enclosing a memorandum on
                    the subject. Two copies of this memorandum are enclosed.<anchor n="11"
                        xml:id="d10-f3"/> There would seem to be no objection to your handing a copy
                    informally to an appropriate official of the Cuban Government should you at any
                    time deem such action advisable.</p>
                <p>The Department has not yet received final replies from the Departments of
                    Commerce and Agriculture with which it has communicated on this subject.
                    Meantime the proposals of the Cuban Government <pb facs="0114" n="18"
                        xml:id="pg18"/> continue to receive careful consideration. It is desired
                    that facts and arguments on both sides which throw light upon the question
                    whether the convention of 1902 is indeed reciprocal in its actual operation be
                    examined in the most cordial spirit by the two Governments concerned.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Leland Harrison</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d10-f1" n="9" xml:id="d10-f1-content">
                <p>Latter not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d10-f2" n="10" xml:id="d10-f2-content">
